We want to create a Ralio multu-Agent workflow for RevOps.

We’re building TrustMe, a multi-agent finance operations platform on top of Ralio.
Most companies have a messy “money-out” problem. Money leaves the business for many reasons: employee reimbursements, supplier invoices, customer refunds, partner commissions, goodwill credits, and one-off operational payments. But these requests usually arrive through Slack, email, spreadsheets, accounting tools, CRM notes, and support tickets. The result is slow approvals, duplicate payments, missed reimbursements, unclear ownership, and poor audit trails.
TrustMe gives the company one intelligent Money-Out Command Centre.
A user can create or upload a payment request, such as: “Reimburse Sarah £38.40 for a client lunch”, “Pay Northstar Design £420 for invoice INV-2042”, or “Refund BrightPath £260 because we overbilled them”. TrustMe then uses a team of specialist agents to investigate the request, decide whether money should move, and send approved payments to Ralio for safe execution.
The first agent is the Orchestrator Agent. Its job is to understand the request and route it to the right specialist. It classifies the request as an employee reimbursement, supplier invoice, customer refund, partner commission, or risky/duplicate case.
Then we have specialist agents.
The Reimbursement Agent handles internal employee expenses. It checks whether the employee exists, whether a receipt is attached, whether the category is allowed, whether the amount is under the auto-approval threshold, and whether this expense has already been claimed.
The Supplier Invoice Agent handles vendor invoices. It checks whether the supplier is known, whether the invoice number already exists, whether the amount matches a purchase order or expected service, and whether approval is required before payment.
The Customer Refund Agent handles revenue-side reimbursements: overbilling, SLA credits, campaign under-delivery, duplicate charges, or goodwill refunds. It checks the customer record, invoice, contract terms, support ticket, previous payments, and calculates the refund amount.
The Partner Commission Agent handles referral, affiliate, or partner payouts. It checks whether the deal closed, whether the commission terms apply, whether the payout amount is correct, and whether it has already been paid.
Finally, the Risk and Duplicate Agent runs across every request. It looks for duplicate receipts, duplicate invoices, repeated payment attempts, suspicious recipient changes, policy violations, and cases that require manual approval.
The important technical point is that these are ReAct agents. They do not just produce a final answer from one prompt. Each agent follows a reasoning loop: it thinks about what it needs to know, calls tools, observes the result, updates its reasoning, and then decides the next action. For example, the Supplier Invoice Agent might first inspect the invoice, then call a supplier lookup tool, then check previous payments, then check policy, then decide whether to approve, block, or escalate.
The UI is designed like a modern finance operations cockpit. The main screen is a Money-Out Inbox showing every request, its type, amount, assigned agent, risk level, decision, and Ralio payment status. A finance or ops user can click into any case and see the full reasoning trail: what the agent checked, which tools it called, which policy rules passed or failed, and why the final decision was made.
Each case detail page has four sections: the original request, the agent investigation, the policy and risk checks, and the Ralio execution panel. If a request is approved, TrustMe sends it to Ralio. If it needs approval, it waits. If it is a duplicate or risky, it is blocked before money moves.
Ralio is the payment execution layer. TrustMe decides whether a payment should happen; Ralio handles the safe movement of money with controls, approvals, status updates, and auditability.
The demo shows four outcomes from one inbox: an employee reimbursement that gets approved and paid, a supplier invoice that requires approval, a customer refund that is calculated and paid, and a duplicate request that is blocked.
So the core idea is simple:
TrustMe is the business brain for money-out operations. It investigates why money should move, and Ralio safely moves it.

Introduction
Ralio
Ralio lets you create AI agents that perform banking operations on your behalf. Each agent operates within guardrails you define: spend limits, tool permissions, and human-in-the-loop approvals. You interact with agents via a web console, CLI, REST API or MCP Gateway.

What Ralio Does
A Ralio agent is a scoped payment authority. You create an agent, configure what it can do, connect it to a banking provider, and then direct it in natural language — via chat or programmatic access. The agent executes banking operations, and Ralio enforces your guardrails at every step.

Typical use cases:

An internal payments bot that processes expense reimbursements after human approval
An automated treasury agent that sweeps balances on a schedule
A customer-service agent that issues refunds within a daily spend limit
An AI assistant in your existing app that can make payments via the MCP Gateway
At a high level, a request flows from any interface to your Ralio agent. The agent operates with scoped authority — bounded by guardrails and approvals, it can only reach payment infrastructure within those limits, and every step is recorded:

